1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:00,280
So

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:06,160
imagine your ministry just rolled out a, a, a flawless digital infrastructure for education.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:08,860
Okay, sounds like a dream for any program director.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:09,160
Right.

5
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:10,980
The system is running beautifully,

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,880
but then your minister calls you in and says, "Great job.

7
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:18,780
Now take that exact same blueprint and apply it to health,

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,220
or maybe agriculture or social protection."

9
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:25,500
Yeah, that is a, well, a very common scenario.

10
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:26,700
And honestly,

11
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:34,600
if I'm a busy minister, my first instinct is just to, you know, copy-paste the whole education app, slap a health logo on it, and call it a day.

12
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:40,260
But taking a successful digital roadmap and forcing it onto a completely different field,

13
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:42,240
it's not that simple, is it?

14
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,200
No, it's definitely not. And that instinct to just copy-paste, um, that's exactly where ministries get trapped.

15
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:57,820
What we really need to figure out is, uh, which parts of a digital roadmap are actually universal across your programs and which parts have to be completely rebuilt from scratch.

16
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:02,680
Right, because if I'm trying to adapt an education blueprint for a ministry of health,

17
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:05,420
some things absolutely have to stay the same.

18
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:07,700
So what actually transfers over?

19
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:14,020
Well, the crucial thing to understand is that the core method transfers perfectly, not the specific software, but the method.

20
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,840
Specifically, um, three foundational things stay exactly the same.

21
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:19,960
First, you keep the five domains.

22
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:20,920
And those are?

23
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:27,040
That's your governance and law, access, digital data, interoperability, and digital identity.

24
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:28,120
Okay, got it.

25
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:33,800
Second, the nine steps of the assessment process, from framing all the way to adopting the roadmap.

26
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:35,000
Those stay identical.

27
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:38,640
And third, your foundational building blocks remain in place.

28
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:41,540
Wait, hold on. The foundational building blocks stay?

29
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:49,620
Why does that work? Because managing student grades seems, I mean, fundamentally different from tracking hospital beds or

30
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:50,660
crop yields.

31
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:56,860
It is different, but let's look at the United Nations Development Program's framework for, uh, digital public infrastructure.

32
00:01:57,340 --> 00:02:04,480
They put three universal things at the absolute core: digital identity, digital payments, and consent-based data sharing.

33
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:05,700
Oh, I see.

34
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:15,200
Think about it. Whether you're a patient paying a clinic or, you know, a student proving your enrollment, the underlying mechanisms of proving who you are and handling a transaction,

35
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:21,580
they are identical. The new sector just builds its specific applications on top of that existing universal core.

36
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:25,160
That makes total sense. Uh, it's kind of like a city grid.

37
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:30,420
The, uh, the roads and the plumbing, like the identity and payments, they stay exactly the same.

38
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,900
But the buildings you put on top, like a school versus a hospital, those change.

39
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:35,800
Precisely.

40
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:43,760
You do not want to pay to rebuild your underlying infrastructure every single time a new department in your ministry digitizes.

41
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:49,400
But here's where I get tripped up. A hospital obviously doesn't operate anything like a school, so

42
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:52,000
where does this blueprint actually diverge?

43
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:53,760
Like, if the core is the same,

44
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:55,520
what am I paying to build?

45
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:59,580
You're paying to build the sector-specific register and its services.

46
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:07,660
Okay. In education, your whole system revolves around a learner register, but in health, you need a register of providers and medical facilities.

47
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:11,560
Or if it's social protection, you're building a social insurance register.

48
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:15,200
You have to assess those specific needs completely afresh.

49
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:21,500
Okay, but if I'm swapping out the core data being tracked, how does the underlying architecture handle that?

50
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:27,980
Well, the reference architecture dictates that digitizing any state registry requires two specific building blocks:

51
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:31,060
registration and a digital registry. Right.

52
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:35,320
So whatever your new sector is recording, it uses those exact same two blocks.

53
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,580
Your new health sector doesn't invent a new way to register people.

54
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,520
It just pays to connect its specific data to those foundational blocks.

55
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,860
I see. It's basically plug and play at the, uh, registry level.

56
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:49,480
But what if a ministry just ignores this?

57
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:55,040
Like, what is the actual danger if they just copy the whole thing wholesale and skip the fresh assessment?

58
00:03:55,740 --> 00:04:00,480
That is the ultimate trap. You have to carry over the method, not the results.

59
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:09,820
If you don't assess the new sector afresh, your systems end up fragmented, siloed, duplicated, rekeyed, locked into one supplier, or, you know, just

60
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:11,700
incredibly costly to change.

61
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:16,760
Because the pain points of a hospital are never going to perfectly mirror the pain points of a classroom.

62
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:20,200
Exactly. To see why this matters, let's look at Progressa.

63
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:25,480
And just to be clear, Progressa is a completely fictional country used in these architectural examples.

64
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,360
Right, just a model for us to look at.

65
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:27,740
Yes.

66
00:04:28,420 --> 00:04:34,340
So when Progressa ran their education assessment, they found that a missing learner record was holding back three different domains.

67
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:37,600
So fixing that learner register became priority number one.

68
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:45,360
But if the health ministry ran its own fresh assessment using those same nine steps, they might uncover a totally different bottleneck.

69
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:49,500
Like maybe their biggest issue is securing a link to the National Identity Authority.

70
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:54,520
Spot on. You can't fix a health identity problem with an education record solution.

71
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:58,520
So to summarize the golden rule for anyone managing these programs,

72
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:03,400
keep the five domains, keep the nine-step method, and keep the foundational blocks.

73
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:09,440
But you absolutely must rebuild the sector's register and services based on a brand-new assessment.

74
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:10,680
That's it exactly.

